---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import AuthorityFlow from "../components/AuthorityFlow.astro";
---
<Site>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="hero wrap" aria-labelledby="headline">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Verifiable human authority for AI agents</p>
      <h1 id="headline">Approve the commitment.<br><em>Not every step.</em></h1>
      <p class="intro">Agents can act. Who gave them the authority?</p>
      <p class="hero-detail">We’re building an open-source foundation for bounded human authority.<br>Permission that travels with your agent, verified before the commitment.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://github.com/vollmachtio/vollmacht">Explore the project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#how-it-works">See how it works <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
      <p class="hero-status">Open source · Early development · Built in the open</p>
    </section>
    <section class="principle-band" aria-label="Design principles">
      <div class="wrap"><p>Agent autonomy.<br><strong>Human authority.</strong></p><p>Bound the action.<br><strong>Not the ambition.</strong></p><p>Verify the permission.<br><strong>Then commit.</strong></p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="vision wrap" aria-labelledby="vision-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">The system we are building toward</p><h2 id="vision-title">Your authority.<br>Across agents and services.</h2></div>
      <AuthorityFlow />
      <p class="caption"><a href="/architecture/">Explore service-side verification and gateway adoption →</a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="workflow wrap" id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="flight-title">
      <div class="section-top"><h2 id="flight-title">A flight, from search to purchase.</h2><span class="eyebrow">Illustrative workflow</span></div>
      <div class="flight-flow">
        <div class="routine">
          <p class="permission"><span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Within existing permission. No new prompts.</p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Search flights</h3><span class="step-icon" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span><p>Find routes that fit<br>your plans.</p></li>
            <li><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Compare options</h3><span class="step-icon" aria-hidden="true">≋</span><p>Weigh price, timing<br>and flexibility.</p></li>
            <li><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Prepare itinerary</h3><span class="step-icon" aria-hidden="true">▤</span><p>Bring the best option<br>back to you.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="commitment">
          <span class="step-number">04 · Approval boundary</span><h3>Buy the ticket</h3>
          <p class="route">SFO <span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only">to</span> JFK</p>
          <p class="fare">$480 total · Nonrefundable</p><p class="scope">One purchase · Expires in 2 minutes</p>
          <div class="approval-example">Approve with passkey <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div>
          <p class="example-note">Example only. No purchase or approval is performed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="workflow-note">Your policy defines when fresh approval is required. Routine work stays within the permissions you already granted.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="examples wrap" aria-labelledby="examples-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">The same idea, beyond travel</p><h2 id="examples-title">Autonomy with a clear boundary.</h2></div>
      <div class="example-grid">
        <article><span class="category">01 / Engineering</span><h3>Ship code.</h3><p>Let an agent read code, run tests and prepare a pull request within its assigned scope.</p><p class="boundary"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> Ask before a protected merge.</p></article>
        <article><span class="category">02 / Data</span><h3>Keep what matters.</h3><p>Let an agent inspect and organize permitted data. Set a separate boundary for irreversible changes.</p><p class="boundary"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> Ask before permanent deletion.</p></article>
        <article><span class="category">03 / Spending</span><h3>Choose, then commit.</h3><p>Let an agent research and compare. Reserve approval for a purchase outside its existing authority.</p><p class="boundary"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> Ask before a financial commitment.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="caption">Illustrative integrations in the target design. Each service applies its own policy; existing authority determines whether fresh approval is needed.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="concept wrap" aria-labelledby="mandate-title">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">A small permission. A precise purpose.</p><h2 id="mandate-title">A mandate,<br>not a blank check.</h2><p>In the Vollmacht design, a Human Mandate binds an agent to a specific action, resource and set of limits. The receiving service or trusted gateway checks that authority before committing the action.</p><p>Vollmacht is German for authority to act on someone else’s behalf. That is the idea: delegated authority, not unrestricted access.</p></div>
      <div class="receipt"><div class="receipt-title">Human Mandate <span>Concept</span></div><dl><div><dt>Agent</dt><dd>Your travel assistant</dd></div><div><dt>Action</dt><dd>Purchase selected ticket</dd></div><div><dt>Resource</dt><dd>Exact reviewed itinerary</dd></div><div><dt>Limit</dt><dd>$480 total · Once</dd></div><div><dt>Validity</dt><dd>2 minutes</dd></div></dl><p>A different itinerary or higher price falls outside this permission.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="security wrap" id="security" aria-labelledby="security-title">
      <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Designed for meaningful oversight</p><h2 id="security-title">Less interruption.<br>More intentional approval.</h2></div>
      <div class="security-grid"><article><h3>No prompt for every step.</h3><p>Repeated prompts invite reflexive approval. The goal is to ask at selected consequential boundaries, not turn every tool call into a biometric ceremony.</p></article><article><h3>Your biometrics stay local.</h3><p>Passkeys use Touch ID or another supported verification method. Vollmacht does not need your fingerprint. User verification is not proof of humanity or proof that you understood a request.</p></article><article><h3>Enforcement matters.</h3><p>A mandate only helps when consequential actions pass through a trusted enforcement layer. It cannot stop an agent that can bypass that layer with its own credentials.</p></article></div>
    </section>
    <aside class="project-status wrap" aria-labelledby="status-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">Available today</p><h2 id="status-title">An open project.<br>A bigger possibility.</h2></div><div><p>The source is public. Explore the design, inspect the experiments, and help shape the path from human approval to service-side enforcement.</p><p>Vollmacht is in early development. This site presents the vision, not shipped merchant or payment integrations. Do not use it as the sole control for production-critical operations.</p><div class="actions project-actions"><a class="button" href="https://github.com/vollmachtio/vollmacht">View the source ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/quickstart/">Start exploring →</a></div></div></aside>
  </main>
</Site>
